								<div class="title" id="post-title-4806162">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/suspicious-mr-guy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3481354); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3481354&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795094" alt="Suspicious Mr Guy" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3481354)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/suspicious-mr-guy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3481354); return false;" >Suspicious Mr Guy</a></span> <span class="odate time_1603903763 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Oct 2020 16:49</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4806162">
									

<p>It didn't catch my attention right away. &quot;Anomalous city-state that makes a lot of amnestics&quot; is kind of interesting as a concept, and was decently executed here, but the initually description isn't particularly thrilling.</p>
<p>The log feels like the main draw of the article, the meat of it if you will, but it does seem to drag on for too long. It contains some nice descriptions and interesting worldbuilding, but I can't help but feel it's be more enjoyable and better paced if compressed down to something like 9 or 10 logs instead of 15. The &quot;transcripts&quot; within the logs read strangely. It doesn't feel like how anyone would write about their day, and it's far too specific. If those were formatted as attached recordings they'd make more sense.(&quot;What do you mean?&quot; He said. Instead of - What do you mean? - He said.) And despite the logs being numbered as if they had unseen logs written between them, they don't read like it. They only ever seem to reference the logs we've seen. It isn't too much of an issue, but it's a bit less immersive than it could be.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">I&nbsp;warned&nbsp;ya</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In 52, when he describes something in incredible detail then says &quot;Oh it was all just a dream&quot; is&#8230; off? It would read better if he described it, mentioned taking pills at the end, and then the next log had a line at the beginning about not remembering the previous log at all or being in a state of delerium during its writing. As it's currently written, it feels too <em>real</em> and <em>specific</em> in its description for something that's supposed to be half-remembered as a dream after taking amnestics.</p>
<p>The whole &quot;it was a nazy town and now they're trying to forget&quot; twist wasn't&#8230; really that much of a twist? Despite the amount of redactions, it was super obvious right from the moment he found the swastika. I don't think the redactions imrpove the feel of the logs, and it's almost always very obvious what's behind those black boxes anyways. It makes the city feel incompetent if this is all they could do to the logs, or if it was the foundation redacting them, why on earth would they black out direct nazi references but leave more than enough context clues to make it <em>very clear</em> what they're hiding?</p>
<p>And the little note at the end doesn't need to exist, or at least doesn't need to exist where it does, since the foundation is already established as currently being in trade relations with the city at the time of the article's writing, which is clearly <em>after</em> the logs arrived. I think the trade relations with the city should be outlined in the containment procedures, not the description, and certainly not in a nonsensical collapsible note at the end. And if the Ethics committee would really take objection to the foundation dealing with a state that <em>used</em> to be a nazi state and is trying to forget it, I guess the foundation can't ever work with the modern German government because <em>they</em> used to be nazis too, you know.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, it's clear there's a lot of potential and ideas here, but the flaws, to me, outweigh the interesting parts.</p>
<p>I rate -1 with a strong encouragement to the author to keep writing and improving. They can't all be winners.</p>

							</div>

								
					
