				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/a-random-day" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1841781); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1841781&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795094" alt="A Random Day" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1841781)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/a-random-day" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1841781); return false;" >A Random Day</a></span> <span class="odate time_1604268782 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Nov 2020 22:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4809268">
									

<p>The translation falls completely flat because it relies so heavily on the diary entries to build a slow-burning mood and atmosphere. This could only work if the diary entries maintain and intensify the same tone throughout. But they can't. Lines like</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This morning I awoke not in my bed in Zone 16, but in a small flat with a very old-timey outfit.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Today it's kinda violet.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>My curator said that's just working gas from the overworked pneumatic system and that there's nothing to worry about</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>This is it - the socialist utopia. Are you sure I'm not in the GDR? No, it's far too backwards - no Trabants, no Spreewaldgurkens.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>continually deflate the tone by sounding incongruous with the rest of the piece. This, combined with a revelation about amnestics that is functionally the same as in <a href="/scp-2419">SCP-2419</a> and <a href="/scp-3000">SCP-3000</a>, ruin an otherwise interesting take on a dystopian bureaucracy. You could use that as the core of a fresh article outright: for example, a country whose entire citizenry is employed in its government, that tax, pay, and delegate each other to make money by facilitating yet other global black market transactions around the world &#8212; and are thus hired frequently by the Foundation. That would be much more interesting than this extremely bloated attempt at dystopia saddled with a cliche story twist about amnestics.</p>

							</div>

								
					
